  "Question": "The equation \\(x^3+6x^2+11x+6=0\\) has:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "no negative real roots",
    "B": "no positive real roots",
    "C": "no real roots",
    "D": "\\(1\\) positive and \\(2\\) negative roots",
    "E": "\\(2\\) positive and \\(1\\) negative root"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The positive coefficients let us rule out positive roots without solving the cubic. For every \\(x\\geq 0\\), the variable terms are nonnegative, so\n\\[\nx^3+6x^2+11x+6\\geq 6>0.\n\\]\nThus no nonnegative number can satisfy the equation. There is a negative real root, since substituting \\(x=-1\\) gives\n\\[\n-1+6-11+6=0.\n\\]\nThis also rules out the choices claiming no negative roots or no real roots. The equation has no positive real roots, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
